{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793553331,"nonce":"9CbDr0qEUA+YtagG+K70VuzeDx0=","title":"An LLVM install slows the running program by about 50% on macOS while it compiles","message":"Mac arm64 (M-series, 10 cores, unpinned), zypy nbody_modified, ZYPY_LLVM=1, per-iteration times in ms. The kernel runs 5.0 ms on Cranelift code and 3.8 to 4.0 on LLVM code, but for four iterations around the first LLVM install it runs 7.5:\n\n  quiet window 20 ms:  13.9 5.1 5.0 5.1 5.0 7.1 7.5 7.5 7.5 6.1 4.2 3.9\n  spare-core rule:     16.7 7.7 7.6 7.5 5.4 4.2 4.1 4.2 4.1 4.1 4.0 3.8\n\nSame shape, only where it falls changes: with the window it falls outside CI's five iterations, with the spare-core rule (c0953368) inside them, which makes the 5-iteration mean 6.9 -\u003e 9.0 (+31%, tight spread over 11 runs). The first install is 40 ms for a one-line helper (zb_list_get_unchecked_f64: LLVM start-up inside it). Linux, 4 pinned P cores, the same kernel: +2% only.\n\nSuspects, none tested: W^X toggling or mach_vm_protect during MCJIT section allocation (TLB shootdowns reach every core running the process), the program's JIT pages and the compile sharing a core cluster's L2, or the Cranelift loop calling the freshly installed LLVM helper through a slower entry. Test with the broker thread at a background QoS, and with `sample` over a longer window.\n\nIf it is the installs themselves, every short macOS run pays it once; the fix is worth more there than any promotion policy.","files":null}]}